## Issue 2 — Public API Endpoints Expose Unpublished Content

### Claim

Any authenticated user, including `guest`, can retrieve metadata and navigation item objects for unpublished pages through API endpoints that require only `public:read`. The same URLs return 404 on the public frontend.

### Code Verified

**Route file:** `system/typemill/routes/api.php` lines 122–125

```php
$group->get('/article/items',    ControllerApiGlobals::class . ':getItemsForSlug')->add(new ApiAuthorization($acl, 'public', 'read')); # guest
$group->get('/article/item',     ControllerApiGlobals::class . ':getItemForUrl')->add(new ApiAuthorization($acl, 'public', 'read')); # guest
$group->get('/article/content',  ControllerApiGlobals::class . ':getArticleContent')->add(new ApiAuthorization($acl, 'public', 'read')); # guest
$group->get('/article/meta',     ControllerApiGlobals::class . ':getArticleMeta')->add(new ApiAuthorization($acl, 'public', 'read')); # guest
```

`public:read` is granted to the `guest` role in `system/typemill/settings/permissions.yaml`, so any authenticated user passes the middleware.

**File:** `system/typemill/Controllers/Controller.php` lines 217–245

```php
protected function contentIsAllowed($username, $userrole, $pagemeta)
{
    $allowedrole = !empty($pagemeta['meta']['allowedrole']) ? $pagemeta['meta']['allowedrole'] : null;
    $alloweduser = !empty($pagemeta['meta']['alloweduser']) ? $pagemeta['meta']['alloweduser'] : null;

    if($alloweduser)
    {
        ...
    }

    if($allowedrole)
    {
        ...
    }

    return true;  # ← defaults to allow
}
```

`contentIsAllowed()` only evaluates the optional `alloweduser` / `allowedrole` restriction fields. It never checks whether the page is published or present in the live navigation tree. Endpoints that rely solely on it therefore leak unpublished items.

**Endpoint details:**

| Endpoint | File:line | Current check | Missing check |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /api/v1/article/item` | `ControllerApiGlobals.php:131–172` | None | Live status + `contentIsAllowed` |
| `GET /api/v1/article/items` | `ControllerApiGlobals.php:174–219` | None | Live status + `contentIsAllowed` |
| `GET /api/v1/article/content` | `ControllerApiGlobals.php:221–315` | `contentIsAllowed` only | Live status for the default path; ownership for `draft=true` |
| `GET /api/v1/article/meta` | `ControllerApiGlobals.php:317–380` | `contentIsAllowed` only | Live status |

`ControllerApiGlobals::getItemForUrl()` and `getItemsForSlug()` both load the **draft navigation** (`getFullDraftNavigation`) which contains `status == 'unpublished'` items. They return those items without any authorization gate.

### Root Cause

The public/external API was designed to mirror the public frontend, which uses the live navigation and returns 404 for unpublished pages. The controller logic never enforces that same “published only” rule. `contentIsAllowed()` is intended for restricting **published** pages to specific users/roles, not for authorizing **unpublished** pages.

### Verdict: CONFIRMED

**Severity: Medium**  
An unprivileged authenticated user can enumerate unpublished pages and read their metadata (titles, descriptions, custom fields, URL structure), even when those pages 404 on the public site.

